      <Comment source="SRI, caspi">Phosphatidylserine decarboxylase catalyzes the formation of phosphatidylethanolamine from phosphatidylserine. It plays a central role in phospholipid metabolism and in the interorganelle trafficking of phosphatidylserine. The enzyme uses a covalently bound pyruvoyl prosthetic group, which is thought to act analogously to pyridoxal phosphate cofactor by forming a Schiff base with the amino group of the substrate and then serving as an electron sink to facilitate the decarboxylation |CITS: [2018773]|. Pyruvoyl-containing enzymes are expressed as a zymogen which is processed post-translationally by a self-maturation cleavage called serinolysis. In this process the pyruvoul group is formed from a serine residue, splitting the presursor protein into two parts which become the &amp;alpha; and &amp;beta; subunits. In some cases additional subunits may be involved. The Arabidopsis thaliana genome contains three genes that encode phosphatidylserine decarboxylase enzymes. The three genes were heterologously expressed in yeast and |FRAME: TAX-562|, and their products were functionally characterized. While the |FRAME: AT4G16700| protein localizes to mitochondria, |FRAME: AT5G57190| and |FRAME: AT4G25970| are found in the endomembrane system. Insertional mutants for all three genes were obtained. The single mutants were decreased in PS decarboxylase activity but showed no obvious growth or morphological phenotype. A triple mutant was totally devoid of PS decarboxylase activity, but still contained phosphatidylethanolamine in isolated mitochondria, indicating that some of the phosphatidylethanolamine in Arabidopsis is synthesized by alternative pathways |CITS: [1744964]|.</Comment>
